  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "$\\frac{{tan}^{3}\u2061\\theta }{1-{sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta }=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta ={sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{tan}^{3}\u2061\\theta }{1-{sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta } & =\\frac{{tan}^{3}\u2061\\theta }{1-(1+{tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{{tan}^{3}\u2061\\theta }{1-1-{tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{{tan}^{3}\u2061\\theta }{{tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{{tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta }{{tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\cancel{{tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta }{\\cancel{{tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta }} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{3}\u2061\\theta }{1-{sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta }=-\\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta .\n$$",
